      "content": "That's a very sharp observation about the distinction between being \"elevated through merit\" versus having that \"innate\" sense of command associated with birthright. It's a harsh reality of these legacy circles.\n\nYou mentioned that Terrence might be viewed as an \"outsider\" and Andre might be seen as a \"wild card\" because their styles don't match that specific aristocratic aesthetic. To be quite direct—when we talk about these candidates being seen as \"outsiders\" or lacking that \"natural\" sense of belonging in an old-money environment, are we talking purely about their management styles, or is there a social/cultural perception at play? \n\nIn these high-level circles, does the board effectively use \"leadership style\" as a proxy for whether someone \"belongs\" in the room? I'm trying to gauge if candidates like Terrence and Andre face a fundamental barrier to entry because they don't fit a specific, traditionalized cultural mold of what an elite leader looks like.",
